[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `tests/probe_nan.rs` | diagnostic: identifies which operand each arithmetic site returns on NaN |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every test loads the Rust library through `libloading` and calls its exported
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m symbols; no Rust function is ever called directly, so the `#[no_mangle]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wrappers and the C ABI of every by-value struct argument/return are under test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Feature combinations
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]` section, so the only combinations that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exist are the default and `--no-default-features` (identical). `run_all.sh`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m enumerates them from `Cargo.toml` and runs the full suite plus the symbol diff
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for each; adding features later needs no change to the script.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The one genuinely build-specific behaviour: NaN operand selection
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the only place where the translation had to be pinned to the reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build rather than to the C source.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x86 SSE scalar arithmetic (`addss` / `subss` / `mulss` / `divss`) is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `dst = dst OP src`, and when **both** operands are NaN the hardware returns the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *destination* operand quieted. Which of the two program values is in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m destination register is a register-allocation decision, so for a commutative
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expression like `a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y` it is not determined by the C source at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Measured with `tests/probe_nan.rs` (NaNs carrying distinct payloads, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result names its own source operand):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | build | `c2Dot((N1,N2),(N3,N4))` returns |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------|----------------------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `gcc -O0` (the reference build) | `N4` — i.e. `b.y` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `gcc -O1` / `-O2` / `-O3` | `N1` — i.e. `a.x` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | naive Rust translation (LLVM `-O3`) | `N1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So **no single Rust implementation can match every GCC optimisation level** —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the levels disagree with each other. The translation matches the reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build produced by the documented command:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build \
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   && cmake .. -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so that is an unoptimised
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`-O0`) build. Against it the whole suite passes (71/71 tests); against
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `gcc -O1..-O3` the NaN-vs-NaN comparisons in `tests/nan_storm.rs` diverge, by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m construction.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The pinning lives in `src/lib.rs` in the `addss`/`subss`/`mulss`/`divss`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m helpers. Each call site carries a comment quoting the reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disassembly that fixes its `dst`/`src` assignment. For non-NaN operands the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m helpers are plain `+ - * /`, so no ordinary result is affected — only NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign/payload selection is forced.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that subtraction and division are unaffected: they are non-commutative, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the destination is always the minuend/numerator regardless of the compiler.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Quirks of the C that are reproduced deliberately, not "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2Absv` uses `x < 0 ? -x : x`. Since `-0.0 < 0` is false, `-0.0` is returned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   **unchanged**, unlike `fabsf`. Pinned by an explicit bit-pattern assertion.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2Minv` / `c2Maxv` use `a < b ? a : b`, which returns `b` whenever the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   comparison is false — so NaN propagation differs from `fminf` / `fmaxf`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2Div` computes `a * (1.0f / b)`, not `a / b`; the reciprocal's rounding is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   observable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2AABBtoAABB` and `c2AABBtoPoint` report NaN inputs as *overlapping* /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   *inside*, because all four rejecting comparisons are false.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2CircleToPoint` uses a strict `<`, so a point exactly on the rim is outside.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2RaytoCapsule` writes `*out` (`n = c2Norm(b - a)`, `t = 0`) **before** it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   knows whether it will hit, so `*out` is modified even on the `return 0` path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ERRORS.md` row 28 tests exactly this; a translation that only wrote `*out` on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   success would pass every hit test and fail here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Negative radii work like positive ones (only `r * r` is used), and inverted
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   AABBs / non-convex polygons / non-unit rotations are never validated.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2CastRay`'s `switch` has no `default`, so any out-of-range `int` returns 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   without dereferencing the shape pointer. Verified by passing `B = NULL`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Deliberately not tested
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
